From e90fbcc9cef28ffd613c2e23d4b4c1f26c1d0226 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iamwrm <iamwrm@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 17:57:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] fix(windows): report host terminal theme so inverse-video
 cells render with real colors

TUIs that draw their cursor as reverse video over default colors (pi,
and anything else using SGR 7 with a hidden native cursor) showed a
white block instead of a theme-colored one in every pane rendered for a
Windows client. Two causes:

1. The Windows client never queried the host terminal's default colors
   (OSC 10/11) and dropped any HostDefaultColor/HostColorSchemeChanged
   events, so the server-side pane renderer had no host theme and
   resolved inverse cells against libghostty's fallback palette
   (white-on-black), baking fg=#000000/bg=#ffffff into the frame.

2. Even without a theme, the renderer had no fallback other than that
   concrete resolution.

Client fix: enable the host theme query on Windows. ConPTY passes the
OSC responses back through console input (OpenConsole's input state
machine passes all input-stream OSCs through to the application); the
console reader reconstructs the report sequences and forwards them to
the server as raw ClientMessage::Input bytes - the same shape Unix
clients deliver - so existing servers (including official Linux builds
reached via --remote) parse them with their raw-input framer and update
the client's host theme. On a color-scheme-change report the client
re-queries, mirroring Unix behavior.

Server fix (defense in depth for terminals that never answer the
query): when an inverse cell would need an untrustworthy resolve color
(host theme unknown and the pane app never customized its palette),
emit Modifier::REVERSED with default colors instead of swapping
concrete fallback colors, letting the host terminal reverse its real
palette. Trust rules live in ghostty_resolved_inverse_colors.

Verified against Windows Terminal 1.24 attached to an official herdr
0.7.4 Linux server: a hidden-cursor SGR 7 block now renders with the
host scheme's colors (#CCCCCC/#0C0C0C for Campbell) instead of
white-on-black, confirmed by screenshot pixel sampling.
